-> T26 Phase 1: `x ∈ S` narrows a *scalar*'s domain to S's elements — x stays
-> unresolved (possibly-many) until narrowed further to a single value.
-> `=` is the singleton case of the same rule: `x = v ≡ x ∈ { v }`.

-> Narrowing directly against a set literal.
x ∈ { 1, 2 }
x = 2
assert x = 2

-> Narrowing against a named set variable (the general-expression fallback —
-> `∈` isn't limited to bare type names; a lowercase identifier holding a Set
-> value works exactly like a set literal would).
ml = { 1, 2, 3 }
y ∈ ml
y = 2
assert y = 2

-> Narrowing further with a second `∈` (or `>`/`<`) intersects — a value not
-> in the original set is provably impossible, same as any other constraint
-> chain. `z ∈ {1,2,3}; z ∈ {2,3,4}` can only be 2 or 3.
z ∈ { 1, 2, 3 }
z ∈ { 2, 3, 4 }
assert z ≠ 1
assert z ≠ 4

-> A ValueSet combined with a range constraint narrows further, using the
-> same intersection machinery as everything else — {1,2,3} ∩ (x > 1) is
-> {2,3}, entailing x < 4 without ever resolving x to one value.
w ∈ { 1, 2, 3 }
w > 1
assert w < 4

-> ...and can fully resolve when the intersection lands on one value.
v ∈ { 1, 2, 3 }
v > 2
assert v = 3

-> The legacy `in [array]` form (pre-T26) still works unchanged.
leg in [10, 20, 30]
leg = 20
assert leg = 20
